but first let's go straight to central  London now and speak to the housing  secretary Robert generic thank you very  much for being on the program this  morning mr. generator woody now the  Prime Minister has said that the NHS  will be overwhelmed unless people start  so she's socially distancing right now  saying that people shouldn't go and see  their mums on Mother's Day it's a pretty  bleak warning what exactly is it that we  all need to do well the figures that  we're seeing coming out of countries  like Italy are very sobering and we all  now need to follow the medical advice so  that we can turn the tide on this virus  the advice is clear we need to stay at  home to protect the NHS and in doing so  we will save lives and so today we're  taking a further very significant step  the chief medical officer has identified  a group of individuals across the  country for specific medical reasons  underlying health conditions are  particularly at risk of coronavirus and  we're writing to these people that's  around 1.5 million people in England and  we're asking them as soon as practicable  to stay at home and to do so for a  prolonged period perhaps as long as 12  weeks and I know if this is you and  you're listening to the program you will  be worried by this and I know you'll be  thinking about the practicalities how  can you get access to food and to  medicines and remain in contact with the  outside world and so we want you to know  that you might be saying at home for a  long period of time but you will not be  alone we're going to do whatever it  takes to support you do you need to be  at home on your own if you're one of  these people identified  well if you have a partner or a person  that you live with then you'll continue  to live at home in the usual setting  we're not expecting families to be  broken up as a result of this but this  will be a big challenge for these  individuals we appreciate that and  that's why for those people who don't  have family and friends and neighbors at  their side to help them we're putting in  place other measures so the NHS will be  delivering medicines to these people  through the community pharmacy network  and I've been working with councils with  supermarkets and with the Armed Forces  to ensure that food and other basic  supplies can be delivered to the  doorstep of these people hundreds of  thousands of people across the country  for as long as it takes  some people be wondering why the  government hasn't stepped in on food  supplies a bit earlier I mean we've all  seen the empty shelves and supermarkets  will struggle to get the online  deliveries yesterday the government said  that a billion pounds we have a billion  pounds more food in our homes than we do  usually why is this only happening now  well I've seen what it's like in  supermarkets and I can only imagine how  it feels if you've come off a long shift  in the NHS or working in social care and  you go to the supermarket and see that  there is enough food in the system we  have a very resilient food sector but we  all need to behave responsibly and  considerately  that isn't happening in all cases today  and we need to make sure that happens  supermarkets are best place to take  action and put in place specific systems  and schemes in their own stores knowing  their customers and what's going on on  the ground and many are doing that  helping the elderly - you have specific  times a day to go shopping or have  special schemes in place for NHS and  social care workers and I strongly  support that but I really urge members  of the public to behave responsibly if  you just shop for you and your family  needs there will be plenty of food for  other people and their families we've  been very clear this morning on what you  should do if you're at this at-risk  category and what I just want to explore  now  it's what everybody else should do what  we should all do because I feel like  some of the messaging has been slightly  confused Forrest Johnson today saying  that you shouldn't be visiting your mum  on Mother's Day today but then on Friday  he was asked if he would see his mother  on Mother's Day and he said he'd  certainly be sending her my very best  wishes and hope to get to see her how is  the public's supposed to know what to do  when the messaging seems to be changing  on a daily basis well we're always  following the expert medical advice and  we have access to the best advice in the  world and we're implementing the  different measures in our action plan at  the right time for the UK the advice  today is very clear we need to stay at  home all by our the most essential  activities and we by doing that will  protect the NHS and help to save lives  so I know it's Mother's Day and I know  it's tough and it goes against  everything that we want to do as a  family but don't go and see your mum  give her a phone call Skype her FaceTime  her tell her that you love her and that  we're going to get through this together  you see I can't help thinking that it  feels as if we are paying playing  catch-up here we only ordered pubs  restaurants cafes to close on Friday  schools were just shut this week and yet  we've known about coronavirus in China  since January we've seen what's  happening in Italy Italy could say that  they didn't know what was coming but we  don't have that excuse so why were these  things not implemented sooner well from  the outset of this crisis we set out all  of the measures that are available to us  in our action plan and those are very  similar measures to other countries that  the other countries have implemented but  we said that we would follow the expert  advice and we would bring each one in  turn into place at the right moment for  this country different countries are at  different stages and the nature of the  virus means that it will take some time  a couple of weeks before we start to see  the effect of the measures that we have  implemented and the ones that we're  implementing now yesterday or Friday the  prime minister announced that bars pubs  and clubs those places where people come  together to meet others in a very social  setting  should now close and that's happened and  today we're saying that this group of  particularly vulnerable people  this 1.5 million individuals should now  stay at home for the duration until  we've turned the tide on the virus I  think the advice is very clear to  members of the public today unless  you're a key worker unless you're going  to work to do something that's  absolutely essential you should now be  staying at home and in doing so we will  protect the NHS we will help to flatten  the curve and will save lives you say  that you should now be staying at home I  just want to ask on behalf of parents of  young children for example how practical  is that I used to label to take your  child to the playground for example well  we've said that you have to balance  staying at home with continuing to stay  fit and healthy so you can go out for  walks you can enjoy the countryside  you can try to to stay fit and healthy  by exercising but you have to do that in  a very responsible way and that means  staying a few meters out that means  staying a few meters away from other  people it doesn't mean having play dates  with other children or coming into a lot  of social contact with others so I think  parents will have to balance this  carefully people shouldn't be crowding  together in parks you are better off  outside then you would be inside but we  all have to behave very responsibly  whilst we're doing this but this is  going to be for quite a long period of  time and so it's important that people  do try to get some exercise and to stay  healthy but to do so following the clear  public health England guidance is it  acceptable to take my child to a  playground or do we not know the  guidance that we've set out says that  you can do exercise you can go for walks  you can go to a playground but you  shouldn't be trying to get into contact  with other people and other children so  if you're doing it try to have make your  children stay a couple of metres away  from others and behaviors responsibly as  they can do lots of people sat out  together in parks or on beaches and so  on that isn't sensible we strongly  discourage that but people going out for  walks  their children just as a family unit  staying where for possible a couple of  metres away from others that's fine and  it's probably the right thing to do  because you've got to maintain as fit  and health as we can do join this period  okay it's interesting hey and were you  talking about strongly discouraging  people from doing things because despite  the prime minister pleading with people  not to do it we've all seen those images  of people going out for a last pint on  Friday night  the reports of hundreds of thousands of  people visiting skegness on Saturday are  you worried that people are not behaving  responsibly and if people continue to do  so could we see a lockdown similar to  those experienced in other countries in  Europe well we don't want to go down  that route you know we want to live in a  free society where we can continue to go  about activities whilst following the  medical advice but this isn't again this  is very serious people need to follow  that advice we have now closed the pubs  the restaurants the clubs the indoor  gyms and leisure activities and we want  people now to follow that advice and  stay at home wherever possible anything  don't if people then clearly will have  to consider if people don't follow that  advice will clearly have to consider  other options but none of us want to go  down that route okay now I just want to  talk about the National Health Service  so we were talking earlier in the  interview about the situation in Italy  you may have seen the graph that the FT  is producing showing that deaths are  happening happening at a quicker rate in  the United Kingdom even than in Italy  where over 4,000 people have already  died the prime minister said today the  Italians have a superb healthcare system  yet their doctors and nurses had been  overwhelmed is our NHS ready  well I want to thank everybody who's  working in the NHS and in social care  and in the other frontline areas of  government like my own in in local  government where local officers are  working around the clock to put in place  the measures that they need this is  going to be a huge effort it's going to  require a heroic national effort really  to get us through this capacity is  increasing the NHS is discharging  patients to build more capacity our call  to arms for people who've left the NHS  to come back is succeeding within just  the first 48 hours of that we've seen  thousands of doctors and nurses coming  forward and that's fantastic we signed a  really important deal with the private  sector yesterday which will bring  forward thousands of more hospital beds  and almost 20,000 additional health care  professionals and put those together at  the service of the NHS as one national  team so we're doing all the things we  can but let's be clear the numbers are  rising we need to follow the the advice  of the healthcare professionals and it's  only by doing that if everyone plays  their part that will begin to turn the  tide  how about ventilators that's another  important part of the fight and last  Sunday that hand Court told me that we  had 5000 but needed many times that have  we made a progress that we have since we  made the call to British industry last  week we've been overwhelmed by offers of  support and there's now a number of  manufacturers who are working with us we  were in receipt of some of the first  prototypes and we expect that  manufacturing can start quite quickly  we're up to I think 12,000 ventilators  now the deal with the private sector  brings an additional 1,000 ventilators  to it to bear on the situation 