recedented economic support package  particularly aimed at people who are  working for employers that will be hit  by coronavirus but lots of people have  pointed out that it doesn't really seem  to do very much for the 5 million people  who are self employed in the United  Kingdom many of them will be really  worried about their jobs can we expect  more help for self-employed people in  the coming days  well we published an unprecedented set  of measures last week which is a very  comprehensive set of measures to support  businesses to support families and  individuals we appreciate that it's not  perfect and there may be more things  that we need to do the Chancellor is  keeping it constantly under review and  is considering what more we might be  able to do for the self-employed but we  have taken steps to support them whether  that's through injecting 7 billion  pounds more into the welfare system  whether it's through Universal Credit or  employment support and other things that  we're doing as well for example helping  to give them more cash flow by  supporting them with their mortgage  holders or if they're renters it is more  complicated for the self-employed than  it is for employees it is logistically  operationally very difficult to  replicate a scheme like we've done for  employed individuals the 80 percent  salary guarantee up to two and a half  thousand pounds a month  but there if if we need to do more we  will do it and the Chancellor's keeping  this under review

